UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 1: Consumer choices', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A need is something required for health and basic participation, such as food, shelter, and a way to get to school. A want is something you would like but can live safely without, such as a branded drink or a new game. The same object can sit in either group depending on the situation: a jumper is a need on a cold morning and a second fashionable jumper may be a want. Sorting needs and wants is the first consumer skill because money spent on a want is then unavailable for a need.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nSort these into need or want for a student who already owns one warm jumper: a second hoodie with a logo, bus fare to school, a new phone case, and a packed lunch.\n\nFEEDBACK\nBus fare and packed lunch are needs for getting to school and eating. The logo hoodie and phone case are wants if a jumper and a working phone already exist.\n\nWATCH FOR\nCalling every purchase a need because you feel like having it. Feeling is not the same test as health or being able to take part in school.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nList four things your household might buy this week. Mark two needs and two wants, and write one sentence on what happens if the wants are bought first. Use examples other than Ari’s poster.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Commerce","learning_intention":"A need is something required for health and basic participation, such as food, shelter, and a way to get to school. A want is something you would like but can live safely without, such as a branded drink or a new game. The same object can sit in either group depending on the situation: a jumper is a need on a cold morning and a second fashionable jumper may be a want. Sorting needs and wants is the first consumer skill because money spent on a want is then unavailable for a need.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CO5-DEC-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: List four things your household might buy this week. Mark two needs and two wants, and write one sentence on what happens if the wants are bought first. Use examples other than Ari’s poster. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using need on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Calling every purchase a need because you feel like having it.. Correction: Bus fare and packed lunch are needs for getting to school and eating.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Calling every purchase a need because you feel like having it. Feeling is not the same test as health or being able to take part in school. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating need on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with need, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about need with 