ty or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check facts, acknowledge limits of the evidence, and avoid inventing details","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete"],"question":"Which action keeps sources, places or historical/geographic evidence accurate for need?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Calling every purchase a need because you feel like having it","Name want only, with no linked example","List four things your household might buy this week","Retype the worked example with no new example"],"question":"Which independent response best applies need to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about need with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating need on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using need on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for need. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with need, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Calling every purchase a need because you feel like having it.. Correction: Bus fare and packed lunch are needs for getting to school and eating.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-commerce-y9-w01-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Year 7 Commerce alignment to Commerce 7–10 (2024) outcome CO5-DEC-01. The syllabus says Stage 4 may adjust Stage 5 outcomes (CO4-ADJ-01). Content-point mapping pending. Not NESA verified. Not financial advice.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise need.","Read the mission before you start mapping or using sources.","Keep the place, people or source you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the source or place detail that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore sources or respectful practice.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread names, places, dates and sequence.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact source detail or place feature before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key evidence.","Say what each source or place detail shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or use of sources, not only how neat the poster looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, person or source that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new sources or places."]},"subject":"Commerce","success_criteria":["I can use need with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify want and explain why it matters.","I can practise choice independently on a new prompt.","I can notice priority needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"A need is something required for health and basic participation, such as food, shelter, and a way to get to school.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","A need is something required for health and basic participation, such as food, shelter, and a way to get to school","A claim unrelated to the source or place"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about need?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nAri has $20. Lunch for the week is not yet packed, and a poster is on sale for $18. Lunch is the need. The poster is a want. Buying the poster first would leave $2, which does not cover lunches. The decision puts the need first.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSort these into need or want for a student who already owns one warm jumper: a second hoodie with a logo, bus fare to school, a new phone case, and a packed lunch.\n\nFEEDBACK\nBus fare and packed lunch are needs for getting to school and eating. The logo hoodie and phone case are wants if a jumper and a working phone already exist.","explain":"A need is something required for health and basic participation, such as food, shelter, and a way to get to school. A want is something you would like but can live safely without, such as a branded drink or a new game. The same object can sit in either group depending on the situation: a jumper is a need on a cold morning and a second fashionable jumper may be a want. Sorting needs and wants is the first consumer skill because money spent on a want is then unavailable for a need.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Calling every purchase a need because you feel like having it. Feeling is not the same test as health or being able to take part in school. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Lunch is the need and the poster is the want when only $20 is available.","caption":"Put the need first when money cannot cover both.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Need</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">lunch for the week</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Want</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">$18 poster</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Ari has $20. Lunch for the week is not yet packed, and a poster is on sale for $18. Lunch is the need. The poster is a w Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which source or place detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Ari has $20. Lunch for the week is not yet packed, and a poster is on sale for $18. Lunch is the need. The poster is a w Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim","A claim unrelated to the source or place"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about need?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nAri has $20. Lunch for the week is not yet packed, and a poster is on sale for $18. Lunch is the need. The poster is a want. Buying the poster first would leave $2, which does not cover lunches. The decision puts the need first. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the HSIE idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show HSIE thinking. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"After an $18 poster, $2 remains, which does not buy the week of lunches.","caption":"Spending the want first removes the need.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">$20</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">poster takes $18</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Left</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">only $2</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual sources and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no evidence","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison","A claim unrelated to the source or place","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about need?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCalling every purchase a need because you feel like having it. Feeling is not the same test as health or being able to take part in school. Here is a more productive model: Ari has $20. Lunch for the week is not yet packed, and a poster is on sale for $18. Lunch is the need. The poster is a want. Buying the poster first would leave $2, which does not cover lunches. The decision puts the need first.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nBus fare and packed lunch are needs for getting to school and eating. The logo hoodie and phone case are wants if a jumper and a working phone already exist.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific HSIE error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Calling every purchase a need because you feel like having it. Feeling is not the same test as health or being able to take part in school. Correct the claim or use of sources, not just the neatness of the poster.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_in